      "updated_at": "2026-09-26T14:03:29Z",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "concept ACK\r\n\r\n---\r\ncould we handle the \"_no timeout_\" case using the OS primitives' native\r\nindefinite timeout, while keeping this cap for explicitly specified large\r\nfinite timeouts ?\r\n\r\nsince `poll()` supports a negative timeout and `select()` can take `NULL`, that\r\nwould preserve the `-rpcclienttimeout=0` semantics without removing the\r\nprotection against very large finite values\r\n\r\nreferences:\r\n\r\n- Linux [`poll(2)`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/poll.2.html)\r\n- FreeBSD [`select(2)`](https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?query=select&sektion=2)\r\n- OpenBSD [`select(2)`](https://man.openbsd.org/select.2)\r\n- NetBSD [`select(2)`](https://man.netbsd.org/select.2)\r\n- Windows [`select()`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winsock2/nf-winsock2-select)\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n>  i do not have an Apple machine to check the macOS local manpages. so macOS users\r\ncan just check the authoritative local versions with `man 2 select` and `man 2\r\npoll`",
      "user": {
        "login": "winterrdog",
        "id": 87463258,
        "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjg3NDYzMjU4",
